  "requestTimestamp": "2026-07-19T01:32:35.890Z",
  "answer": "### Short Answer\nIn addition to filing the annual income tax return, taxpayers must submit supporting documents such as financial statements, proof of income, and any other relevant documentation as required by the Bureau of Internal Revenue (BIR).\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nThe primary authority governing the filing of income tax returns and the required documents is found in Section 51 of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC), which outlines the requirements for individual returns.\n\n### Interpretation\nSection 51 of the NIRC specifies that individuals are required to file an income tax return and outlines the necessary documentation that must accompany the return. This includes financial statements and proof of income, which serve to substantiate the figures reported in the return.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nTaxpayers should ensure that they gather and submit all necessary documents along with their income tax return to avoid issues with the BIR. Proper documentation not only supports the accuracy of the return but also helps in case of audits or inquiries from tax authorities.",
